{"format_version":"vbulletin-3+-thread","url":"https://forum.theonyxpath.com/forum/main-category/main-forum/the-new-world-of-darkness/hunter-the-vigil/390334-on-the-rating-of-monsters-cr-for-nwod-theory","id":390334,"title":"On The Rating Of Monsters [CR for NWOD; THEORY]","path":["Home","Onyx Path","White Wolf Worlds","Chronicles of Darkness","Hunter: The Vigil"],"posts":[{"date":"2015-02-02T14:23:03-08:00","id":390334,"title":"On The Rating Of Monsters [CR for NWOD; THEORY]","content":"<b>Preface:</b> Yes, I am quite aware there are going to be some people who will <i>immediately</i> say that NWOD does not need CR, because it is a <i>story game you filthy roll-player</i> (exaggeration for humour purposes). However, I must say that I disagree; without such a system, we're relying <i>entirely</i> on ST familiarity with the system, which is a barrier for people who are STing their first game.<br /> <br /> <b>What is CR?:</b> CR was a numerical trait introduced in the third edition of D&amp;D; it was intended to allow GMs to easily construct encounters that would be challenging but doable, without having to spend egregious amounts of time staring at the (quite complicated) character sheets.<br /> <br /> Needless to say, it... didn't work too well. There were low CR monsters that could eat higher level parties <i>alive</i>, while there were high CR monsters that could be dispatched trivially if you had good party tactics. It was clunky, tied to a white-room understanding of the rules, and overly-simplistic.<br /> <br /> <b>What Can We <i>Learn</i> From CR?:</b> Despite that, CR wasn't a bad <i>idea</i>. Compared to, say, Exalted 2e, where there <i>weren't</i> guidelines to help out STs, CR is a <i>wonderful</i> thing, once you've learned that it's a loose guideline (at best.) Additionally, it's something that you can abstract out to cover social interactions, the difficulty of mental endevours, etc, etcetera.<br /> <br /> <b>But How?:</b> The reason that it's... difficult to apply a CR-equivalent to NWOD is that progression isn't linear; while you have a unified &quot;level&quot; system for d20 and derivatives, NWOD progression is more tree-shaped; there's no &quot;automatic&quot; proficiency in something (like how D&amp;D automatically makes you tougher and better at fighting as you gain experience, which works there due to the combat-centric focus of the game.)<br /> <br /> My suggestion? A quick set of categories that are there to help you eyeball how much of a challenge a given &quot;thing&quot; is, as well as a quick listing of particular &quot;angles of attack&quot;. Really, just a way that you can glance at a sheet and go, &quot;Oh, the characters in my group wouldn't be able to survive 5 seconds in a fight with this thing. Maybe I shouldn't plan a fight with this thing.&quot; or &quot;huh, this guy's really good at social stuff, but he's a shitty liar. I should keep that in mind...&quot;","signature":"<hr class=\"b-divider\"/><br/>I have decided, after some thought, that I don't really feel happy on these forums. I might decide to come back to post. Who knows - but right now, I'm gone.<br /> <br /> So good bye, good luck, and have a nice day.","edited":{"userid":0,"user_link":"","user":"","date":""},"attachments":[],"reactions":[{"type":"\ud83d\udc4d","count":2}],"user":{"link":"https://forum.theonyxpath.com/member/1170-amechra","name":"amechra","avatar":"https://forum.theonyxpath.com/core/avatars/0/0/6/0/4/5/avatar1170_2.png","title":"Member","rank":5,"rank-name":"","custom":[{"key":"Join Date:","value":"Nov 2013"},{"key":"Posts:","value":"2316"}]},"thankedby":[]},{"date":"2015-02-02T20:19:57-08:00","id":390912,"title":"","content":"NeoTiamat's Rank system seems like an adequate substitute for this sort of thing.","signature":"<hr class=\"b-divider\"/><br/>I am no longer participating in the community. Please do not contact me about my previous work.","edited":{"userid":0,"user_link":"","user":"","date":""},"attachments":[],"reactions":[],"user":{"link":"https://forum.theonyxpath.com/member/159-leetsepeak","name":"Leetsepeak","avatar":"data:image/svg+xml;base64,","title":"Member","rank":5,"rank-name":"","custom":[{"key":"Join Date:","value":"Nov 2013"},{"key":"Posts:","value":"4259"}]},"thankedby":[]},{"date":"2015-02-02T20:27:03-08:00","id":390918,"title":"","content":"I am flattered to be mentioned, but the Rank thing is a first step at <i>best</i>. It just sets how much XP a character has. How it gets spent is still utterly dependent on the character, and different builds and tactics can have wildly different results.<br /> <br /> I think having a CR is a great idea, but I'm not sure that the White Wolf system is sufficiently non-random for this to really work. Would be awesome if it did.","signature":"<hr class=\"b-divider\"/><br/><b>GM of the <i>Walking Shadow</i> Campaigns</b><br /> <a href=\"http://forum.theonyxpath.com/forum/main-category/main-forum/the-new-world-of-darkness/8992-the-hacks-and-houserules-of-walking-shadows\" target=\"_blank\">New System and Setting Material</a>","edited":{"userid":0,"user_link":"","user":"","date":""},"attachments":[],"reactions":[],"user":{"link":"https://forum.theonyxpath.com/member/1040-neotiamat","name":"NeoTiamat","avatar":"https://forum.theonyxpath.com/core/avatars/0/0/6/0/4/5/avatar1040_2.jpeg","title":"Member","rank":5,"rank-name":"","custom":[{"key":"Join Date:","value":"Nov 2013"},{"key":"Posts:","value":"471"}]},"thankedby":[]},{"date":"2015-02-02T21:35:52-08:00","id":390998,"title":"","content":"I was actually thinking of mentioning it; I just forgot to (sorry NeoTiamat!)<br />","signature":"<hr class=\"b-divider\"/><br/>I have decided, after some thought, that I don't really feel happy on these forums. I might decide to come back to post. Who knows - but right now, I'm gone.<br /> <br /> So good bye, good luck, and have a nice day.","edited":{"userid":0,"user_link":"","user":"","date":""},"attachments":[],"reactions":[],"user":{"link":"https://forum.theonyxpath.com/member/1170-amechra","name":"amechra","avatar":"https://forum.theonyxpath.com/core/avatars/0/0/6/0/4/5/avatar1170_2.png","title":"Member","rank":5,"rank-name":"","custom":[{"key":"Join Date:","value":"Nov 2013"},{"key":"Posts:","value":"2316"}]},"thankedby":[]},{"date":"2015-02-03T01:05:55-08:00","id":391138,"title":"","content":"This sort of things can be tricky, maybe you could do two CR syst\u00e8me, one for the combat ability and the other for the nuisance potential ? A werewolf would be a powerful opponent, but a vampire or changeling pulling strings from the shadow would be a pain to deal with.","signature":"","edited":{"userid":0,"user_link":"","user":"","date":""},"attachments":[],"reactions":[],"user":{"link":"https://forum.theonyxpath.com/member/3883-arabidopsis","name":"Arabidopsis","avatar":"data:image/svg+xml;base64,","title":"Member","rank":0,"rank-name":"","custom":[{"key":"Join Date:","value":"Sep 2014"},{"key":"Posts:","value":"72"}]},"thankedby":[]},{"date":"2015-02-03T07:43:22-08:00","id":391425,"title":"","content":"Well, I was thinking that you'd have loose categories for stuff like social, combat, political, etc. So that werewolf would have a high rating in the combat category while that vampire might have a high social/political.","signature":"<hr class=\"b-divider\"/><br/>I have decided, after some thought, that I don't really feel happy on these forums. I might decide to come back to post. Who knows - but right now, I'm gone.<br /> <br /> So good bye, good luck, and have a nice day.","edited":{"userid":0,"user_link":"","user":"","date":""},"attachments":[],"reactions":[],"user":{"link":"https://forum.theonyxpath.com/member/1170-amechra","name":"amechra","avatar":"https://forum.theonyxpath.com/core/avatars/0/0/6/0/4/5/avatar1170_2.png","title":"Member","rank":5,"rank-name":"","custom":[{"key":"Join Date:","value":"Nov 2013"},{"key":"Posts:","value":"2316"}]},"thankedby":[]},{"date":"2015-02-03T08:12:10-08:00","id":391451,"title":"","content":"Similar to how the Storyteller Adventure System rates things? A scale of 1-5 for each category (Physica/Social/Mental)? I could see that working, and I like it.<br /> <br /> Perhaps something like:<br /> <b>Queen Frenchy-name Fancypants, Vampire Politico</b><br /> Physical 3 - To represent the fact that while it's not her forte, her access to powerful vampire powers and a lot of temporal resources like guns and bodyguards makes her a challenge.<br /> Social 5 - She's can control your mind and read your thoughts! Not to mention all of those politicians, zoning board members, superintendents, and garbage scow crews she has in her pocket that could make your life miserable.<br /> Mental 4 - She's called the Night Queen of Sao Paulo for a reason: she plays dirty and efficiently. Perhaps her pride and paranoia can be used against her?<br /> <br /> Or<br /> <br /> <b>Juniper MacMillon, Heart-Eating Shapeshifter</b><br /> Physical 2 - Supposedly he can turn into a bear, but other than his resistance to bullets he should be easy to take out.<br /> Social 3 - He's situated himself nicely as the friendly family doctor. He offers a useful service cheaply to the community, and has donated some green to the local boys in blue.<br /> Mental 5 - This guy's schedule is hard to track, and it's not as simple as just showing up at his office and blowing him away. Not to mention he's a spry bastard and knows how to run when things aren't going his way.<br />","signature":"<hr class=\"b-divider\"/><br/>&quot;<a href=\"http://forum.theonyxpath.com/forum/main-category/main-forum/the-new-world-of-darkness/werewolf-the-forsaken/127887-doing-first-tongue-translations?p=484226#post484226\" target=\"_blank\">Nihhina kalekal-zidu kal masun, kal manudanadu. Nihhina kalekal-zidu nukal shaghu-desasudu \u2014 nihhina kalekal-zidu kal innu-desasudu udhkal samm</a>.&quot; Arthur Ashe<br /> She/her, contributing writer for <a href=\"https://www.drivethrurpg.com/product/363404/Scion-Pride-2021\" target=\"_blank\">Scion: Pride 2021</a>","edited":{"userid":0,"user_link":"","user":"","date":""},"attachments":[],"reactions":[],"user":{"link":"https://forum.theonyxpath.com/member/1923-bigdamnhero","name":"BigDamnHero","avatar":"https://forum.theonyxpath.com/core/avatars/0/0/6/0/4/5/avatar1923_4.jpg","title":"Member","rank":5,"rank-name":"","custom":[{"key":"Join Date:","value":"Jan 2014"},{"key":"Posts:","value":"1263"}]},"thankedby":[]},{"date":"2015-02-03T08:37:32-08:00","id":391462,"title":"","content":"<div class=\"bbcode_container\"> <div class=\"bbcode_quote\"> <div class=\"quote_container\"> <div class=\"bbcode_quote_container b-icon b-icon__ldquo-l--gray\"></div> <div class=\"bbcode_postedby\"> Originally posted by <strong>amechra</strong> <a href=\"https://forum.theonyxpath.com/forum/main-category/main-forum/the-new-world-of-darkness/hunter-the-vigil/390334-on-the-rating-of-monsters-cr-for-nwod-theory?p=390998#post390998\" rel=\"nofollow\" title=\"View Post\" class=\"b-icon b-icon__double-arrow-right--blue animate-scroll\">View Post</a> </div> <div class=\"message\">I was actually thinking of mentioning it; I just forgot to (sorry NeoTiamat!)</div> </div> </div> </div>Well now I'm doubly flattered. So I'll throw out a couple of thoughts, unfortunately mostly ones about how this would be a difficult thing to do (I'll note that I have GMed campaigns in both D&amp;D 3.5 and D&amp;D 4E, and I am fond of the system -- I think the D20 system and the Storyteller system have <u>different</u> strengths and weaknesses, and each one does certain things better than the other). I'll also note that this applies to NWoD 1E, as I never upgraded the combat system for GMC (we were all used to the old one and weren't convinced that the new one was sufficiently superior to be worth disrupting the campaign, redoing the stats, and learning the rules).<ul><li>The biggest issue I can see is that the Storyteller System is deeply <i>random</i>. Compare to D20 -- there you roll a single die and add a static modifier for most things. In the Storyteller system, it's <i>all</i> dice rolls, which means that the range of possible outcomes is much higher. You can get seven successes on ten dice, or two successes on fifteen dice, and so forth. This makes encounters much harder to 'predict' in any fashion -- your punk neonate might out-talk the Prince who's been politicking since Machiavelli was in diapers if he gets enough exploding dice.</li> <li>The modularity of the system means that there is nothing that makes certain that characters will be more or less even across the board in a given area. You can have a sky-high Persuasion score and an untrained Subterfuge, you might have 15 health and a Defense of 2. Compare to D&amp;D, again, where most of numbers scale with level -- you might focus more or less on something, but the differences between your worst and your best are nowhere as extreme. This makes it harder to 'reduce' any complex set of stats down to a quick number.</li> <li>Exchanges of dice rolls tend to be quick. Look at combats. The average battle in D&amp;D 3.5 when I was running it was 5 to 7 rounds, in 4E, it was more like 8+. I have <i>rarely</i> had a combat in the Storyteller system last longer than 3 rounds. Characters simply aren't that durable. This means that a small number of dice rolls can have a proportionately greater impact -- the battle doesn't last long enough for there to be a regression to the mean. This again makes encounters harder to predict. The same applies to social and mental challenges, which again tend to be even faster.</li> <li>Finally, the Storyteller system has a deep fondness for various forms of what in D&amp;D tends to be called &quot;Save-or-Die,&quot; that is, supernatural powers that can utterly shut down a specific encounter. In particular, there are lots of mind-controls, lots of fear effects, any of which will completely shut down a battle. And the Storyteller's usual dice pool system, where you are rolling a three-part dice pool (Attribute+Skill+Power) versus a two-part dice pool (Attribute+Power Stat) means that these things will <i>usually</i> succeed. Your punk neonate gets a Dominate off against the 800-year-old Prince, it does not take too much luck to end the battle then and there.</li> </ul>So... I think a CR system would be an <i>extremely</i> good thing. But I'm not sure the Storyteller system is sufficiently predictable for it to work -- both the modularity of character creation and the method of dice rolling works against it..","signature":"<hr class=\"b-divider\"/><br/><b>GM of the <i>Walking Shadow</i> Campaigns</b><br /> <a href=\"http://forum.theonyxpath.com/forum/main-category/main-forum/the-new-world-of-darkness/8992-the-hacks-and-houserules-of-walking-shadows\" target=\"_blank\">New System and Setting Material</a>","edited":{"userid":0,"user_link":"","user":"","date":""},"attachments":[],"reactions":[{"type":"\ud83d\udc4d","count":2}],"user":{"link":"https://forum.theonyxpath.com/member/1040-neotiamat","name":"NeoTiamat","avatar":"https://forum.theonyxpath.com/core/avatars/0/0/6/0/4/5/avatar1040_2.jpeg","title":"Member","rank":5,"rank-name":"","custom":[{"key":"Join Date:","value":"Nov 2013"},{"key":"Posts:","value":"471"}]},"thankedby":[]}]}